  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「タケアズキ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%BF%E3%82%B1%E3%82%A2%E3%82%BA%E3%82%AD",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106366191,
      "text": "タケアズキ （英語:Ricebean） 、カニメ (蟹目)、 バカアズキとも呼ばれる。トウモロコシ、モロコシとの混作で窒素固定を行なうために用いられることが多い。種子は、飼料や食用にされる。中国では主に漢方薬として利用されるが、広東スープの食材としても一般的な為（葛の根と煮る粉葛赤小豆湯など）、広東省ではスーパー等で容易に手に入れる事が出来る。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Vigna umbellata」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vigna_umbellata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1319825428,
      "text": "Vigna umbellata, previously Phaseolus calcaratus, is a warm-season annual vine legume with yellow flowers and small edible beans. It is commonly called ricebean or rice bean. To date, it is little known, little researched, and little exploited. It is regarded as a minor food and fodder crop and is often grown as intercrop or mixed crop with maize, sorghum or cowpea, as well as a sole crop in the uplands, on a very limited area. Like the other Asiatic Vigna species, ricebean is a fairly short-lived warm-season annual. Grown mainly as a dried pulse, it is also important as a fodder, a green manure and a vegetable. Ricebean is most widely grown as an intercrop, particularly of maize, throughout Indo-China and extending into southern China, India, Nepal and Bangladesh. In the past it was widely grown as lowland crop on residual soil water after the harvest of long-season rice, but it has been displaced to a great extent where shorter duration rice varieties are grown. Ricebean grows well on a range of soils. It establishes rapidly and has the potential to produce large amounts of nutritious animal fodder and high quality grain."
    }
